.TH SORT 1.SH NAMEsort \- sort or merge lines of text.SH SYNOPSIS\fBsort\fR[\-cmu] [\-t sep] [\-o file] [\-bdfiMnr] [+pos1 [\-pos2]] [\-k pos1[,pos2]][file ...].SH DESCRIPTION\fBsort\fRis GNU sort, from the textutils.  It sorts the lines of all the namedfiles together, or of the standard input, and writes the result to thestandard output.  Lines compare as whole text unless a key is given..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-c\fRcheck only: say whether the input is already sorted, and exit 1 with a`disorder' message if it is not..TP 12\fB\-m\fRmerge files that are each already sorted..TP 12\fB\-u\fRprint only one of each run of equal lines..TP 12\fB\-o\fI file\fRwrite the result to\fIfile\fR,\fRwhich may be one of the inputs..TP 12\fB\-t\fI sep\fRfields are separated by\fIsep\fRrather than by runs of blanks..TP 12\fB\-k\fI pos1[,pos2]\fRsort on a key from field\fIpos1\fRto field\fIpos2\fR,\fRcounting from 1.  A letter after a position applies an ordering optionto that key alone:\fB\-k 2n\fRis field two, numerically..TP 12\fB+\fIpos1\fB [\-\fIpos2\fB]\fRthe same, the older way, counting from 0:\fB+1\fRis the second field..TP 12\fB\-n\fRnumeric: `10' after `9'..TP 12\fB\-r\fRreverse..TP 12\fB\-f\fRfold lower case into upper..TP 12\fB\-M\fRcompare month names: JAN before FEB..TP 12\fB\-b\fRignore blanks at the start of a key..TP 12\fB\-d\fRdictionary order: only letters, digits and blanks count..TP 12\fB\-i\fRignore characters outside printable ASCII..SH EXAMPLES.nfsort names                        alphabeticalsort -u names                     and without repeatssort -t : -k 2n scores            by the second :-field, numericallysort +1 -n scores                 the same key, the older way, blank-separatedsort -r -o names names            reversed, in placesort -M dates                     by month name.fi.SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOuniq, join, tac